            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-32", "sa-backup-dr", "SQL Server runs on an Azure VM, and the database must be recoverable to within 15 minutes of a failure, with full and log backups managed centrally. What do you recommend?", listOf("Azure Backup for SQL Server in Azure VMs, with log backups every 15 minutes", "A daily VM backup only", "Copy the database files to Blob Storage each night", "Azure Site Recovery"), setOf(0), "Azure Backup has a workload-aware backup for SQL Server in Azure VMs. It takes full, differential and log backups, with log backups as often as every 15 minutes, and restores to a point in time.",
                scenario = "Haverford Clinics' appointments database, used by its Android booking app, runs on SQL Server in an Azure VM. Losing a day of bookings would be a disaster.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "A daily VM backup can lose up to a day of data.",
                    2 to "Copying files of a running database is not a consistent backup, and loses up to a day.",
                    3 to "Site Recovery protects against outages but keeps no backup history for a point-in-time restore.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-33", "sa-backup-dr", "During failover to the other region, a DNS record must be updated and a load balancer reconfigured automatically, between bringing up the database tier and the web tier. What do you use?", listOf("An Azure Automation runbook added as a step in the Site Recovery recovery plan", "A note in the runbook binder", "A second Recovery Services vault", "A resource lock"), setOf(0), "Recovery plans can include scripts or Azure Automation runbooks before or after any group. They run automatically during failover, so steps such as DNS changes happen in the right order without people.",
                scenario = "Glenfield Water's DR test for the back end of its Android outage app took four hours because engineers changed DNS and load balancers by hand in the middle.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Manual steps are what made the test take four hours.",
                    2 to "Another vault adds nothing to the order of failover.",
                    3 to "Locks stop deletions; they do no work during failover.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-34", "sa-backup-dr", "The primary region of a GRS storage account is down, Microsoft has not yet failed over, and the business wants writes back now, accepting some data loss. What do you do?", listOf("Start a customer-managed (unplanned) failover of the storage account to the secondary region", "Wait until the primary returns, whatever it takes", "Restore from yesterday's backup to a new account", "Change the redundancy to LRS"), setOf(0), "With geo-redundant storage you can start an account failover yourself. The secondary becomes the primary and writes resume. Data not yet copied to the secondary, usually the last few minutes, is lost.",
                scenario = "Bracken Couriers' Android proof-of-delivery app writes photos to a GRS account. The primary region has been down for three hours and drivers cannot complete deliveries.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Waiting keeps drivers stuck for an unknown time.",
                    2 to "A day-old backup loses far more than a failover would.",
                    3 to "Changing redundancy while the region is down does nothing to restore writes.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-35", "sa-backup-dr", "A Cosmos DB account must keep periodic backups, but every two hours instead of four, held for a week. What do you do?", listOf("Change the periodic backup interval and retention on the account", "Switch to serverless", "Add a second region", "Use Azure Backup's VM policy"), setOf(0), "In periodic backup mode you can set how often backups are taken and how long they are kept. A restore from them is requested through Microsoft support.",
                scenario = "Hollowell Games keeps player inventories for its Android game in Cosmos DB in periodic backup mode. Losing four hours of purchases is too much, but the team does not need self-service restore.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Serverless changes how throughput is billed, not backups.",
                    2 to "A second region replicates every mistake at once.",
                    3 to "VM backup policies do not apply to Cosmos DB.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-36", "sa-backup-dr", "An Azure SQL database in a failover group must be running in the secondary region within minutes of a regional outage. What should the recovery plan rely on?", listOf("The automatic failover policy, with its default grace period", "A planned failover, which waits until the two databases are in sync", "A manual forced failover started by the team as soon as the outage is confirmed", "A geo-restore of the latest backup"), setOf(2), "A failover group's automatic failover waits for a grace period, an hour by default, before acting, so it alone cannot meet a target of minutes. A forced failover started by the team takes effect quickly, accepting that the last few seconds of data may be lost.",
                scenario = "Galloway Pay's Android wallet must recover within 15 minutes of a regional outage. Its Azure SQL database is in a failover group with a secondary in another region.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    0 to "Automatic failover waits for its grace period, an hour by default, so the 15-minute target would be missed.",
                    1 to "A planned failover needs the primary to be reachable to synchronise, which it is not during an outage.",
                    3 to "Geo-restore takes much longer and loses more data than failing over to the replica.",
                ),
            ),
            // Business continuity: high availability
            B.q("sa-m4-37", "sa-high-availability", "A single legacy VM cannot be clustered. Which choice gives it the best SLA Microsoft offers for one VM on its own?", listOf("Use Premium SSD or Ultra disks for all its disks", "Use Standard HDD disks", "Put it in an availability set on its own", "Turn on boot diagnostics"), setOf(0), "A single VM gets a 99.9 percent SLA when all its disks are Premium SSD, Premium SSD v2 or Ultra disks. Running two or more VMs across zones or in an availability set gives more, but this app cannot be clustered.",
                scenario = "Westmoor Council runs a licensing server for its Android parking app that cannot run as two copies. The council wants the best uptime promise it can get.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Standard HDD gives a much lower single-VM SLA.",
                    2 to "An availability set protects only when it has two or more VMs.",
                    3 to "Boot diagnostics helps troubleshooting. It has nothing to do with the SLA.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-38", "sa-high-availability", "Users in Asia and America read a database whose primary is in Europe, and reads must be fast for them while the copies can also take over in a disaster. What do you recommend?", listOf("Active geo-replication with readable secondaries in Asia and America", "A larger primary in Europe", "Zone redundancy in Europe", "Elastic pools in each region"), setOf(0), "Active geo-replication keeps up to four readable secondary databases in other regions. Readers in each region query their nearest copy, and any secondary can be failed over to if the primary region is lost.",
                scenario = "Riverside Language School's Android course app has learners worldwide, with its Azure SQL database in West Europe. Learners far away find lessons slow to load.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "A bigger primary does not shorten the distance to Asia and America.",
                    2 to "Zone redundancy protects within Europe and adds no copies near the users.",
                    3 to "Elastic pools share resources between databases. They do not replicate data.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-39", "sa-high-availability", "The cache behind the app must survive a data centre failure and also serve users in a second region with local reads and writes. What do you recommend?", listOf("Azure Managed Redis with zone redundancy and active geo-replication", "A Redis server installed on one VM", "An in-memory dictionary in each web server", "Azure Managed Redis with one node and no replication"), setOf(0), "Azure Managed Redis spreads its nodes across availability zones for high availability. Active geo-replication links caches in several regions, each accepting reads and writes, so users get a nearby cache.",
                scenario = "Oriel Games' Android leaderboard reads from a Redis cache. The game is launching in America, and an outage last year emptied the cache for an afternoon.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "One VM is a single point of failure, in one region.",
                    2 to "Separate in-memory copies drift apart and vanish on restart.",
                    3 to "A single node without replication fails with its data centre.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-40", "sa-high-availability", "A Cosmos DB account with one write region and two read regions must lose no committed data if the write region fails. Which consistency level gives that?", listOf("Eventual", "Session", "Strong", "Consistent prefix"), setOf(2), "With Strong consistency, a write is confirmed only when the other regions have it too, so a regional failure loses no committed data. The cost is higher write latency, and it cannot be used with multi-region writes.",
                scenario = "Sterling Exchange's Android trading app records orders in Cosmos DB. The regulator says a confirmed order must never be lost, even in a regional outage.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    0 to "Eventual consistency confirms writes before other regions have them, so recent ones can be lost.",
                    1 to "Session consistency guarantees a user sees their own writes, not that other regions have them.",
                    3 to "Consistent prefix keeps order but still confirms before replication.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-41", "sa-high-availability", "SQL Server must run on Azure VMs, and the database must survive the loss of a data centre with automatic failover. What do you recommend?", listOf("An Always On availability group with replicas on VMs in different availability zones", "One large VM with Premium SSD", "Two VMs in a proximity placement group", "Daily backups to another region"), setOf(0), "An Always On availability group keeps copies of the database on several SQL Server VMs and fails over automatically. Placing the VMs in different availability zones means the loss of one data centre leaves a working replica.",
                scenario = "Marchbank's warehouse system needs SQL Server features that tie it to VMs, and its Android picking app stops if the database stops.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "One VM, however good, fails with its data centre.",
                    2 to "A proximity placement group puts the VMs close together, often in the same data centre.",
                    3 to "Backups take hours to restore and lose recent data.",
                ),
            ),
            // Infrastructure: compute
            B.q("sa-m4-42", "sa-compute", "One web app is already packaged as a single Linux container, and the team wants managed hosting with custom domains, autoscale and slots, without orchestrating containers. What do you recommend?", listOf("Azure App Service for Containers (Web App for Containers)", "Azure Kubernetes Service", "Azure Batch", "Azure Container Instances with no load balancer"), setOf(0), "App Service can run a custom container as a web app, with the platform's custom domains, TLS, autoscale and deployment slots. For one web container there is no orchestrator to learn.",
                scenario = "Bramling's Android gardening app has a single web API container. The two developers have no Kubernetes experience and want staging slots for releases.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "AKS orchestrates many containers and means running a cluster, too much for one container.",
                    2 to "Batch runs compute jobs, not web APIs.",
                    3 to "Container Instances has no slots or built-in autoscale for a web app.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-43", "sa-compute", "Data must stay encrypted even while it is being processed in memory, so that the cloud operator cannot read it. What do you recommend?", listOf("Azure confidential VMs", "Standard VMs with disk encryption", "Spot VMs", "VMs in a proximity placement group"), setOf(0), "Confidential VMs run inside hardware-based trusted execution environments that encrypt memory with keys the host cannot read. This protects data in use, not just at rest or in transit.",
                scenario = "Lanyon Genomics processes DNA data for the reports in its Android health app. Its research partners demand that data be protected even while it is being computed.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Disk encryption protects data at rest, not in memory.",
                    2 to "Spot VMs are about price, not protection.",
                    3 to "Placement groups are about latency, not encryption.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-44", "sa-compute", "Twenty Windows Server VMs run around the clock for the next three years, and the company already owns Windows Server licences with Software Assurance. How do you cut their cost the most? Select two.", listOf("Buy reserved instances, or a savings plan, for a three-year term", "Apply the Azure Hybrid Benefit to use the existing Windows Server licences", "Convert them to Spot VMs", "Turn them off at night"), setOf(0, 1), "Reservations and savings plans give large discounts for committing to steady compute for one or three years. The Azure Hybrid Benefit removes the Windows licence cost from the VM price when you bring licences you already own.",
                scenario = "Pryor Shipping's tracking platform behind its Android app runs on 20 VMs that must stay up all the time. Finance wants the lowest cost for the next three years.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    2 to "Spot VMs can be evicted at any time, which a service that must always run cannot accept.",
                    3 to "These VMs must run around the clock.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-45", "sa-compute", "A serverless function must scale to zero and reach a database that has only a private endpoint in a VNet. Which hosting plan do you recommend?", listOf("Azure Functions Flex Consumption plan with virtual network integration", "Azure Functions Consumption plan on Windows", "A Free App Service plan", "Azure Logic Apps Consumption"), setOf(0), "The Flex Consumption plan scales to zero, bills per use, and supports virtual network integration, so the function can reach private endpoints. The older Windows Consumption plan has no VNet integration.",
                scenario = "Merlin Garages' Android booking app uses a function to check service slots in a private Azure SQL database. Traffic is light, and cost must stay low when idle.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "The Windows Consumption plan does not support virtual network integration.",
                    2 to "There is no Free plan for Functions with VNet integration, and it would not scale out.",
                    3 to "Logic Apps is a workflow service, not where the team's function code runs.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-46", "sa-compute", "A self-managed database on a VM needs a data disk where IOPS and throughput can be raised and lowered independently of its size, without downtime. Which disk type do you specify?", listOf("Premium SSD v2", "Standard HDD", "Standard SSD", "Ephemeral OS disk"), setOf(0), "Premium SSD v2 lets you set capacity, IOPS and throughput separately and change performance on the fly. You pay for the performance you need instead of buying a bigger disk to get it.",
                scenario = "Calder Analytics runs a self-managed database for its Android dashboard app on an Azure VM. It needs high IOPS at month end but only 100 GB of space.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Standard HDD offers low, fixed performance.",
                    2 to "Standard SSD performance is tied to disk size and is too low for this.",
                    3 to "Ephemeral disks are temporary OS disks and lose data on reimage.",
                ),
            ),
            // Infrastructure: application architecture
            B.q("sa-m4-47", "sa-app-architecture", "The back end must push live score updates to 200,000 connected app users at once over WebSockets, without running its own socket servers. What do you recommend?", listOf("Azure Web PubSub", "Azure Storage queues", "Azure Batch", "Azure Data Factory"), setOf(0), "Azure Web PubSub is a managed service for real-time messaging over WebSockets. It holds the client connections and lets the back end publish to all of them, or to groups, at scale.",
                scenario = "Kickoff's Android football app shows live scores while the app is open. The team's own WebSocket servers fall over during big matches.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Queues pass work between back-end services. They do not push to open apps.",
                    2 to "Batch runs compute jobs, not live connections.",
                    3 to "Data Factory moves data in pipelines, not to app users in real time.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-48", "sa-app-architecture", "Some messages carry 20 MB video files, larger than the Service Bus Standard message limit, and the team wants to stay on Standard. What design do you recommend?", listOf("The claim-check pattern: store the file in Blob Storage and send a message with its location", "Split the file across hundreds of messages", "Compress the file inside the message", "Use Event Grid instead"), setOf(0), "The claim-check pattern puts the large payload in a store such as Blob Storage and sends only a small message pointing at it. The receiver fetches the payload, so the message stays well under the limit.",
                scenario = "Reelhouse's Android video app uploads clips that a back end processes in steps through Service Bus Standard, whose messages are limited to 256 KB.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Splitting a file across messages is fragile and hard to reassemble in order.",
                    2 to "Video is already compressed, so it will not shrink enough.",
                    3 to "Event Grid events are small too, and Event Grid is not a work queue.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-49", "sa-app-architecture", "API Management must reject calls without a valid Microsoft Entra access token before they reach the back-end API. What do you configure?", listOf("A validate-jwt policy on the API in API Management", "A subscription key only", "An IP filter allowing all phones", "A rate-limit policy"), setOf(0), "The validate-jwt policy checks the token on each call: its signature, issuer, audience and expiry. Calls without a valid token are rejected at the gateway and never reach the back end.",
                scenario = "Tarnwell Insurance's Android app calls its claims API through API Management. A penetration test found the API could be called with a subscription key copied from the app.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "A subscription key can be extracted from the app and identifies no user.",
                    2 to "Phones have changing IP addresses, so an IP filter cannot tell good calls from bad.",
                    3 to "Rate limiting slows abuse but lets unauthenticated calls through.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-50", "sa-app-architecture", "Product images and scripts are fetched millions of times by users worldwide and change rarely. How do you cut load on the origin and speed up delivery?", listOf("Cache them at the edge with Azure Front Door", "Store them in Azure SQL Database", "Add more App Service instances in one region", "Serve them through API Management with no caching"), setOf(0), "Azure Front Door caches static content at Microsoft's edge locations close to users. Most requests are answered from the edge, so they are faster and the origin sees far fewer of them.",
                scenario = "Bluestone Outdoor's website and Android app load product images from App Service in one region. Users in Asia wait seconds for each image.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "A database is the wrong place for images and is no closer to users.",
                    2 to "More instances in one region do not reduce distance to Asia.",
                    3 to "API Management without caching sends every call to the origin.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-51", "sa-app-architecture", "Developers must create their own short-lived test environments from approved templates, within cost and policy limits, without platform team tickets. What do you recommend?", listOf("Azure Deployment Environments", "Give every developer Owner on production", "Email the platform team for each environment", "One shared test environment for everyone"), setOf(0), "Azure Deployment Environments lets developers create environments themselves from templates the platform team approves, in subscriptions and with settings the platform team controls, and they can expire automatically.",
                scenario = "Cobalt Health's Android app teams wait a week for each test environment. The platform team wants self-service without losing control of cost and security.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Owner on production removes all control and risks production.",
                    2 to "Tickets are the week-long wait the teams complain about.",
                    3 to "One shared environment makes teams trip over each other's changes.",
                ),
            ),
            // Infrastructure: migrations
            B.q("sa-m4-52", "sa-migrations", "The assessment found an old intranet app nobody has used for a year, and a factory control system that must stay on-site for safety reasons. How should each be classed in the migration plan?", listOf("Retire the unused app and retain the factory system on-premises", "Rehost both to Azure VMs", "Refactor both into containers", "Replace both with SaaS"), setOf(0), "Rationalising the estate means deciding what to do with each workload. Retire means switching off what is no longer needed. Retain means keeping on-premises what must stay there for now.",
                scenario = "Aldermoor Engineering is planning its move to Azure, starting with the back end of its Android field app. The assessment turned up 30 workloads, including these two.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Moving an unused app costs money for nothing, and the factory system must stay on-site.",
                    2 to "Refactoring either one is wasted effort for these cases.",
                    3 to "There is nothing to replace for an unused app, and the factory system cannot leave the site.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-53", "sa-migrations", "VMware VMs must be replicated to Azure without installing agents, checked in a test migration that does not touch production, and then cut over. What do you recommend?", listOf("Agentless replication in Azure Migrate, with a test migration before the final migration", "Copy the VMDK files by hand", "Azure Site Recovery to a second VMware site", "Rebuild every VM from scratch"), setOf(0), "Azure Migrate replicates VMware VMs to Azure without agents, using the appliance. A test migration starts copies in an isolated network to check them, and the final migration cuts over when ready.",
                scenario = "Penrose Markets is moving 80 VMware VMs, including the back end of its Android shopping app, and wants to prove each one works in Azure before it switches.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Copying disk files by hand is slow, error-prone and gives no test run.",
                    2 to "A second VMware site is not Azure.",
                    3 to "Rebuilding everything is far more work than replicating.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-54", "sa-migrations", "Forty terabytes of files must reach Blob Storage within two weeks. The office has a dedicated 1 Gbps link with spare capacity. Which approach fits?", listOf("Copy online with AzCopy over the link", "Order a Data Box, because 40 TB is always too much for the network", "Email the files", "Use Azure Site Recovery"), setOf(0), "A 1 Gbps link moves roughly 10 TB a day at full speed, so 40 TB takes about four or five days with overheads, well within two weeks. An online copy with AzCopy avoids the wait for shipping a device.",
                scenario = "Kittiwake Maps stores map tiles for its Android hiking app on a NAS in its office. The link to Azure is new, dedicated and mostly idle.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Data Box suits slow or busy links. Here the network can do it in days.",
                    2 to "Email cannot carry terabytes.",
                    3 to "Site Recovery replicates VMs for disaster recovery, not file copies.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-55", "sa-migrations", "A SQL Server database must move to Azure SQL Database. A four-hour overnight maintenance window is acceptable. What do you recommend?", listOf("An offline migration with Azure Database Migration Service", "Site Recovery replication of the SQL Server VM", "Copy the MDF file into Blob Storage", "Log Replay Service"), setOf(0), "Database Migration Service copies the schema and data from SQL Server to Azure SQL Database. An offline migration, with the source stopped during the copy, is simple and fits when a downtime window is acceptable.",
                scenario = "Hallgarth Library's catalogue database, used by its Android borrowing app, is 50 GB. The library is closed overnight, so downtime then is fine.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Site Recovery moves the VM, not the database into Azure SQL Database.",
                    2 to "Azure SQL Database cannot attach an MDF file.",
                    3 to "Log Replay Service restores backups onto Managed Instance, not Azure SQL Database.",
                ),
            ),
            // Infrastructure: networks
            B.q("sa-m4-56", "sa-network-connectivity", "The data centre needs 100 Gbps of dedicated connectivity straight into Microsoft's network, with link-layer encryption. What do you recommend?", listOf("ExpressRoute Direct with MACsec", "A site-to-site VPN", "ExpressRoute through a provider at 1 Gbps", "Point-to-site VPN"), setOf(0), "ExpressRoute Direct connects you directly to Microsoft's edge routers at 10 or 100 Gbps. It supports MACsec, which encrypts traffic on the physical link.",
                scenario = "Halberd Research moves petabytes of imaging data that feed its Android lab app between its data centre and Azure. Compliance demands encryption on the link itself.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "VPNs run over the internet and cannot reach 100 Gbps.",
                    2 to "1 Gbps is a hundred times too slow.",
                    3 to "Point-to-site connects single devices, not a data centre.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-57", "sa-network-connectivity", "A partner allows calls only from a fixed list of IP addresses, and the company will keep adding public IPs for new services. How do you give the partner one stable range to allow?", listOf("A public IP prefix, with new public IPs taken from it", "Ask the partner to update its list each time", "Use dynamic public IPs", "Private endpoints"), setOf(0), "A public IP prefix reserves a contiguous block of public addresses. Public IPs created from it all fall in the block, so the partner allows the whole range once.",
                scenario = "Torbay Insurance's services behind its Android app call a partner's pricing API. The partner takes weeks to change its firewall list, and Torbay adds services every month.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Updating the partner each time is the weeks-long delay to avoid.",
                    2 to "Dynamic addresses change, which breaks the partner's list.",
                    3 to "Private endpoints are inbound private addresses, not outbound public ones.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-58", "sa-network-connectivity", "The site-to-site VPN must keep working through planned maintenance or failure of one gateway instance, with both tunnels in use. What do you configure?", listOf("An active-active VPN gateway with tunnels to the on-premises device", "A Basic SKU gateway", "A second VNet", "Point-to-site VPN for every server"), setOf(0), "In active-active mode, both instances of the VPN gateway have their own public IP and tunnel. Traffic uses both, and if one instance is down, the other keeps the connection up.",
                scenario = "Harlech Water's control room reaches the Azure back end of its Android outage app over a VPN. Gateway maintenance caused a 90-second drop that set off alarms.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "The Basic SKU does not support active-active.",
                    2 to "Another VNet does not make the gateway more resilient.",
                    3 to "Point-to-site is for single devices, not a site.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-59", "sa-network-optimize", "Very high throughput between on-premises and VMs over ExpressRoute is limited because traffic passes through the virtual network gateway. What do you recommend?", listOf("ExpressRoute FastPath, so traffic goes straight to the VMs", "A bigger VPN gateway", "Azure Front Door", "Add a NAT gateway"), setOf(0), "ExpressRoute FastPath sends traffic from the circuit directly to VMs in the VNet, bypassing the gateway in the data path. This removes the gateway as a throughput limit.",
                scenario = "Ellery Studios renders video for its Android editing app on Azure VMs and moves huge files over ExpressRoute. Transfers peak at the gateway's limit.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
